course: money
lesson: 16
title: In-game currency: coins and gems are real money
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: V-Bucks, Robux, gems and coins are in-game currency: pretend money inside a game, but bought with real pounds, so every gem you spend was once real money.
explain: Games sell coins in packs that never quite match the prices: Ethan buys 1,000 V-Bucks for £7.99, but the skin costs 1,200, so he is 200 short and must buy another pack, spending £15.98 and keeping 800 left over that tempt him to spend again.
explain: Change it back into pounds to see the real price: if 500 gems cost £4.99, each gem is about 1p, so a 2,000-gem item costs about £20.
explain: Games are designed to make you spend: tiny 99p prices that add up, countdown clocks on "special offers" that come back every week, a flashy shop next to the play button, and daily streaks you are scared to break.
fact: In-game currency (coins, gems, V-Bucks, Robux) is pretend money inside a game, bought with real money.
fact: Games sell currency in packs that do not match item prices, so you often buy more than you need.
fact: Changing game money back into pounds shows the real price.
fact: A microtransaction is a small payment inside a game; many small payments add up to a big total.
fact: Countdown timers on offers make you rush; many "limited-time" offers come back again and again.
fact: Games often let you either "grind" (play a lot to earn something) or pay to get it faster; paying is how many game companies earn money.
fact: Most game items cannot be sold for real money, so once bought, the money is gone; they are fun, not savings.
fact: A battle pass is a paid list of rewards unlocked by playing during a season; it is only good value if you play a lot.
fact: Game shops are placed where you see them often, because seeing something often makes you want it more.
fact: Daily login rewards and streaks keep you coming back by making you feel you will lose something if you stop.
fact: Game money works like real money for sums: add the prices, then take them away from what you have.
exercise: What is in-game currency? | Pretend money in a game, bought with real money | Free money that games give everyone | Real coins hidden inside the console | Money the game pays you for playing
exercise: Ethan has 1,000 V-Bucks and the skin costs 1,200. How many is he short? | 200 | 1,200 | 2,200 | 100
exercise: Ethan buys a second £7.99 pack. How much has he spent in all? | £15.98 | £7.99 | £16.98 | £14.98
exercise: With 2,000 V-Bucks and a 1,200 skin, how many are left over? | 800 | 200 | 1,000 | 3,200
exercise: 500 gems cost £4.99. About how much is one gem? | About 1p | About 5p | About 50p | About £1
exercise: If one gem is about 1p, about how much is a 2,000-gem item? | About £20 | About £2 | About £200 | About 20p
exercise: Kian earns 200 coins an hour, or can pay £2 for 200 coins. What are 1,000 coins worth in pounds? | £10 | £5 | £2 | £20
exercise: What does "grind" mean in a game? | Play a lot to earn something | Pay money to skip ahead quickly | Break the controller in a temper | Delete the game and start again
exercise: Amy asks for a 99p pack 12 times in a month. How much is that? | £11.88 | £12.00 | £9.99 | £11.99
exercise: A "4 hours left!" offer of £9.99 appears every week. If Jordan buys it 4 weeks running, how much is that? | £39.96 | £39.99 | £36.96 | £9.99
exercise: Maya has 400 Robux and buys a 75 hat and 250 shoes. How many are left? | 75 | 325 | 150 | 25
exercise: Noah's mate's skin cost £100 but can't be sold. What is it worth in real money now? | £0 | £100 | £50 | £200
exercise: A £7.99 battle pass has 100 rewards. Finn unlocks only 10. About how much did each reward cost him? | About 80p | About 8p | About £8 | About 10p
exercise: Why is the game shop placed right next to the play button? | So you see it every time and want to spend | Because there was no other space left on the screen | So it's easier to find the exit when you finish | Because shops must be near the play button by law
exercise: Isla gets 10 free coins a day for logging in. How many in a week? | 70 | 17 | 10 | 100
exercise: Why do games use streaks like "Don't break the streak!"? | To make you feel you'll lose something if you stop | To help you sleep on time | To give you real money | To stop you playing too much
test: Ethan goes "1,000 V-Bucks and the skin's 1,200? That's a con, bro." Is the pack size an accident? | No, packs are made so you buy more than you need | Yes, games never think about pack sizes when making them | Yes, it's a bug the game will fix | No, it's so you get free V-Bucks
test: Jordan panics at "SPECIAL OFFER — 4 hours left!" His sis: "chill, they do that every week." What's the clock for? | To make you rush instead of thinking about the price | To help you plan your homework | To show the real end of the game | To count how long you've played
test: Amy whines "it's only 99p, Mum!" for the twelfth time this month. What does Mum point out? | All those small buys add up to nearly £12 | 99p buys are free in the end, once you level up | 12 times is still only 99p in total, all together | Gems get cheaper and cheaper the more you buy
test: Noah's mate brags "my skin's worth like £100, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) Noah asks "can you sell it?" "Nah." What's it really worth? | Nothing in real money, though it was fun | Exactly £100, sitting in the bank for later | £200, because it's one of the very rare skins | £50, which is exactly half of what he paid
test: Uncle Pat, a Dublin man, says "they put the shop there so ya see it every single time, ya eejit." ("Eejit" means idiot, said fondly.) Why? | Seeing something often makes you want it more | The shop is where the game saves your progress | It's the only place it fits on the screen | So you can find the settings
test: Maya has 400 Robux. Hat 75, shoes 250, and — oh wait — the shoes are on sale at 200. How many left after both? | 125 | 75 | 325 | 275
test: Kian: "grind or pay, innit." 5 hours of play earns 1,000 coins, or he pays £10. What's the trade-off? | His time against his money | His coins against his skins | His password against the game | Nothing, because both of them are free
test: What would happen if a game showed prices in pounds instead of gems? | People would see the real cost and might spend less | Everything would become cheaper | Gems would be worth more | Nothing at all would change
test: Finn bought the £7.99 battle pass but played 3 days and the season ended. His mate says "W pass, so worth." ("W" means a win.) Was it? | No, he unlocked few rewards, so it was poor value | Yes, battle passes are always worth it | Yes, it carries over to every season | No, battle passes are always free
test: Isla breaks her streak after 6 days of 10 coins and it resets to 0. How many coins did she collect in those 6 days? | 60 | 70 | 16 | 0
test: Lily's bro: "500 gems for £4.99, so 1,500 gems is like a fiver." What does 1,500 gems really cost? | About £15 (£14.97) | About £5 | About £1.50 | About £50
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises in-game currency? | A notional currency within a game, acquired in exchange for real money | A currency issued by the central bank for use in games | A currency which may invariably be redeemed for cash | A currency furnished without charge to all players
exam: It is given that one thousand units of game currency are priced at seven pounds ninety-nine pence. Compute the expenditure incurred in the purchase of two such packs. | Fifteen pounds ninety-eight pence | Fifteen pounds ninety-nine pence | Sixteen pounds ninety-eight pence | Seven pounds ninety-nine pence
exam: It is given that a player holds four hundred units and purchases articles of seventy-five and two hundred and fifty units. Ascertain the balance remaining. | Seventy-five units | One hundred and fifty units | Three hundred and twenty-five units | Twenty-five units
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a countdown timer displayed upon an offer within a game. | To induce haste and thereby discourage consideration of the price | To inform the player of the time remaining before bedtime | To indicate the duration of play hitherto completed | To guarantee that the offer shall never recur
